The '''1998 [[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]] gubernatorial election''' was held on November 3, 1998. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] incumbent [[Zell Miller]] was unable to seek re-election due to term limits. In the open race to succeed him, Democratic state representative [[Roy Barnes]] defeated Republican businessman [[Guy Millner]].

[[Georgia House of Representatives|State representative]] [[Roy Barnes]] won the Democratic Party's nomination after a close and highly contested primary election, while businessman [[Guy Millner]], who had run for governor and the [[United States Senate]] in the previous four years, won the nomination of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]].

In the general election, Barnes was able to defeat Millner by a margin of victory larger than Governor Miller's victory over Millner four years prior, which was in part due to the unpopularity and controversy of [[Mitch Skandalakis]], the Republican nominee for [[Lieutenant Governor of Georgia]]. This was the last time a Democrat was elected Governor of Georgia until [[2026 Georgia gubernatorial election|2026]].

==Democratic primary==

===Candidates===
* [[Roy Barnes]], [[Georgia House of Representatives|state representative]] from [[Marietta, Georgia|Marietta]]
* [[Lewis A. Massey]], [[Georgia Secretary of State|Georgia secretary of state]]
* [[David Poythress]], Georgia commissioner of labor
* Steve Langford, [[Georgia State Senate|state senator]] from [[LaGrange, Georgia|LaGrange]]
* Morris James
* Carlton Myers, [[Pine Mountain, Harris County, Georgia|Pine Mountain]] veterinarian and [[1974 United States Senate election in Georgia#Democratic primary|1974 candidate for Senate]]

